illion in wage subsidies  plus 1 billion announced in the fall  economic statement for airports and  smaller airlines  analysts say barring another negative  turn of events the worst of the  turbulence is behind the industry  ann gaviola global news toronto  still ahead we hear from a former  interpreter for canadian troops who says  he's being  hunted by the taliban  translators who worked side by side with  canadian soldiers in afghanistan keep  pleading for help because they worked  with foreign forces they're considered  traitors by some in their country and  live in fear of being attacked or killed  by the taliban  the federal government knows lives are  hanging in the balance  mike armstrong reached one man who says  the taliban are searching  for him the afghan military has been  losing ground  for weeks when it does the taliban moves  in  as they do they have targets yeah i was  in a  bad place we spoke to a former afghan  interpreter who worked with canadian  troops  in 2010 and 2011. last week he was in a  town  outside kandahar where protecting his  identity he says the taliban was  after him they already have information  about the people who work with the  coalition forces or  metal when they enter to the city  they were going directly to the house  home and take out him from the home  and they kill in front of their parents  of their family now that interpreter's  been moved with his wife and daughter  where exactly we've been asked not to  say  only that it's a safer place and a  location that'll make it easier to get  them out of the country eventually  but that family is one of close to two  dozen being helped by a group of retired  canadian  soldiers they've been organizing  fundraising and calling in favors  to help people who used to work with  them we are not going to quit  retired corporal robin rickards is part  of the network he says he's been living  on three hours of sleep for days  and he's lost track of how many people  have jumped in with support  there's lots of people pitching in where  they uh where they can and doing what  they  what they're able to now there is  frustration with the federal government  while ottawa has said it's working on a  plan that's about all it said  there's no word on who'd qualify and  there's no timeline  these are people who believed in canada  one voice speaking out on the issue is  catherine rusk  she is the sister of captain nicola  goddard canada's first female soldier to  be killed in combat  rusk says her sister's interpreter is  still in afghanistan  i don't think canadians are okay with it  i don't think canadians are okay with  leaving  the people who have helped us behind now  a potential  airlift would likely be out of kabul but  there was a sign this week the capital  is getting less and less  safe these were rockets  landing near the presidential palace  during prayers tuesday  with each day that passes there is an  increasing urgency  to get interpreters out mike armstrong  global news  next the women to watch as canada  competes in an olympics  like no other  it's official brisbane australia will  host the 2032  summer olympic games it is the first  city to win a summer bid  under a new selection process the ioc  overhauled the process in 2019 to  discourage  cities from competing against each other  in expensive bidding wars  and competition is underway at the tokyo  games team canada is starting on the  right foot  our softball team clinched a shutout win  in their opening round  led by a pitcher who three years ago  wasn't sure she'd live to see these  games  crystal gomencing reports health  concerns have plagued the tokyo  2020 games but sarah gronawagen proved  she is in top form clinching a shutout  victory in the team's tournament debut  after battling back from a near-death  experience  with legionnaires disease the win all  the sweeter as  softball just returned to the games for  the first time in  13 years we've got a wonderful mix of  youth and experience a great chemistry  our championship culture women who are  highly committed to their craft  and we're going to win a gold medal  whether that happens or not remains to  be seen but that's our intention  canadian women are also once again  leaving their mark on the pitch  the women's soccer team is striving to  meddle in its third  consecutive olympics six minutes in  christine sinclair put canada on the  board this was her 187th goal in  international competition  then came a heart-stopping moment  canada's keeper edmonton's  stephanie lebaye clearly in pain  but unwilling to leave the match her  dedication  and skill shone through there were no  fans in the stadium but odds are  any canadian watching let out a cheer  with that save  le bay did ultimately leave the match  and japan would later tie it up  for a 1-1 draw the sapporo dome was also  the site  of the first political statements of the  games soccer players from team great  britain new zealand and  others took a knee before their matches  players  had been told such symbolic gestures  would be prohibited at these games  but when asked the ioc president  softened that stance  it is allowed it's no violation of the  rule 50. this is  expressively what has been mentioned in  these guidelines  thomas bach also found himself defending  covet 19  protocols and protections there are now  more than  75 covet 19 infections among athletes  and accredited games personnel  tokyo recorded its highest daily case  count in  six months with more than eighteen  hundred new infections  a reminder of the unrelenting realities  of this pandemic  and the backdrop of the most unusual  olympics  crystal gomencing global news london  and that is global national for this  wednesday i'm donna friesen tonight's  air canada  is the victorian row houses known as  jelly bean row in st  john's newfoundland and labrador thanks  for watching hope to see you here again  tomorrow  take care of yourselves bye-bye
 

